ewitness news eight  the lake is rising the good beaches and  the boat ramps are disappearing  underwater this is not a boat ramp it's  a parking lot with a lake rising you  pick a good parking spot when you leave  for that weekend trip or you're the  instant owner of a submarine just a few  days ago rangers were still optimistic  not many are showing much concern about  launching the boats because the park has  stated we'll make every effort to keep  the launch ramps themselves open  and here we can do it we can  use the parking area that was a few days  ago now rangers are stationed at the  launch sites to regulate traffic on what  is left of the launch ramps the high  water has crested completely over the  break water at echo bay workers are  laboring feverishly to build the  breakwater above the rising lake level  without the breakwater millions of  dollars worth of boats at echo bay would  be violently slammed about in the event  of high winds and the rising water has  changed the complexion of the lake the  old salts that know the lake like the  back of their hands now have new  obstacles to contend with the shoreline  there will be real problems when  officials begin lowering the lake level  the shoreline which has been under water  for days will then be exposed that is  expected to play havoc with a bass bond  we'll have more on fishing a major  attraction of the lake tomorrow mark  fiero eyewitness news eight  if you're thinking about fishing on lake  mead this weekend don't let the high  water stop you there are a few anglers  out there who may blame their bad luck  on the rising lake level but park  officials say the numbers just don't  bear out the negative claims  it isn't bothering the fishing a bit  creole census taker was here today and  he's reporting about what he always does  the fishermen are still out and still  catching the fish lest you think that's  just some massaging from the bureaucrats  the locals say the same sparky perkins  grew up in nearby overton he says the  rising water may help it's lowering the  lake that may throw a wrench into your  creel i know that it'll be good for  fishing it'll make fishing real good i  know that uh in the past times every  time they raise the lake they lower the  lake and it ruins  the bass spawn and it could have a great  effect as far as  if there was to take and drop the lake  clear back down right now  but lowering the water is not the  immediate problem for now the lake  continues to rise a favorite fishing  spot for some black canyon directly  behind the dam has now been closed to  boaters completely and bureau of  reclamation officials have now revised  their estimates of just where the lake  will peak by another two and a half feet  these pictures were taken saturday this  concession stand at overton beach is now  half underwater federal officials now  expect water to begin pouring over the  spillways at hoover dam either late  sunday or early monday morning that's  the first time that has happened since  1941. mark fierro eyewitness news eight  only once before has water seeped over  these spillways that was in 1941 when  the government successfully tested their  operation  but this year the bulging colorado river  filled the lake mead reservoir and the  water came tumbling down  the site brought three times the number  of visitors to the dam and tourism  officials are expecting record crowds  over the next few weeks as the cascading  waters rise another three or four feet  i think it's amazing i've never seen  anything like it before  neither have residents downstream who  complain that this site is proof the  bureau of reclamation mishandled control  of the colorado this spring  john pilot a boulder city resident was  one of the original workers on the dam  project he saw the spillways operate in  1941 and he returned to the dam to watch  them again  well i'm just sorry to see that it  happens  personally  because a control of the flood waters is  in real bad danger at this time  water flow through the dam's power  plants must now be reduced to compensate  for this spillage bureau of reclamation  officials say the overflow could lead to  a complete shutdown of hoover's  generators in the next few weeks that  would be the first shutdown in the  plant's 47 year history  but officials are hoping that won't  happen they will try to maintain a  steady 40 000 cubic feet per second flow  of water from the dam and say that early  signs are encouraging  well at this time we're hopeful to keep  the  increases if any at the downstream level  to a minimum we do know that the inflow  is dropping it's a little early to tell  but at this point it looks encouraging  officials will collect data over the  next few days to determine the extent of  water releases and although the swollen  colorado river poses no threat to the  structure of hoover dam it could  continue to take its toll on  flood-ravaged communities downstream  cindy benson eyewitness news 8.

